\title{Denominators of Rational Points on Mordell Curves:\\
The ``Only Bad Primes'' Conjecture is False}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $N$ be a nonzero integer and let $E_N : y^2 = x^3 + N$ be the associated Mordell curve, whose discriminant is $\Delta = -432N^2$, so that the primes of bad reduction are exactly the divisors of $6N$. A folklore conjecture---attractive because of its cryptographic consequences when $N = pq$ is a semiprime---asserts that every prime occurring in the denominator of the $x$-coordinate of a multiple $nP$ of a rational point $P \in E_N(\Q)$ is a prime of bad reduction, i.e.\ lies in $\{2,3\} \cup \{r : r \mid N\}$. We prove that this conjecture is false, and we determine completely the mechanism that makes it false. We establish: a square--cube rigidity theorem ($\den(y)^2 = \den(x)^3$, hence points are of the shape $(a/e^2, b/e^3)$ with $b^2 = a^3 + Ne^6$); a reduction dichotomy ($\ell \mid \den(x)$ if and only if the point lies in the kernel of reduction at $\ell$, a condition independent of the discriminant); an explicit counterexample criterion (an integral point with $\ell \mid y$, $\ell \nmid 6N$ forces the good prime $\ell$ into $\den(x(2P))$); the counterexample $N = 55 = 5 \cdot 11$, $P = (9,28)$, with $x(2P) = 2601/3136$, $3136 = 2^6 \cdot 7^2$, where $7$ is a good prime and $\gcd(3136,55) = 1$; an infinite family $N = \ell^2-1$, $P = (1,\ell)$ with $\den(x(2P)) = 4\ell^2$ exactly; and exact valuation dynamics along doubling orbits. These results replace a false heuristic with a complete description, and identify a precise obstruction to using Mordell-curve denominators for integer factorisation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The conjecture and its appeal}

The Mordell curve
\[
E_N : \quad y^2 = x^3 + N, \qquad N \in \Z \setminus \{0\},
\]
is an elliptic curve over $\Q$ with discriminant
\[
\Delta(E_N) = -432 N^2 = -2^4 \cdot 3^3 \cdot N^2 .
\]
Its primes of bad reduction are precisely the primes dividing $6N$. When $N = pq$ is a semiprime the bad primes are exactly $\{2,3,p,q\}$.

The rational points $E_N(\Q)$ form a finitely generated abelian group under the chord-and-tangent law with identity the point at infinity $O$. Multiples of a non-torsion point have coordinates whose denominators grow doubly exponentially, and those denominators are large integers manufactured by a process in which $N$ is a visible parameter. This motivates the following.

\begin{conjecture}[Only Bad Primes]
Let $N = pq$ and let $P \in E_N(\Q)$. Then every prime $\ell$ dividing the denominator of $x(nP)$ divides $\Delta = -432N^2$; equivalently $\ell \in \{2,3,p,q\}$.
\end{conjecture}

Were this true, computing $\gcd(\den x(2P), N)$ after removing powers of $2$ and $3$ would exhibit a nontrivial factor of $N$, yielding a polynomial-time factoring algorithm for semiprimes carrying a known rational point on the associated Mordell curve.

\subsection{What we prove}

We disprove the conjecture in the strongest available senses: by a four-digit counterexample; by a criterion showing counterexamples are generic; by an infinite explicit family; and by a complete determination of the valuation dynamics, showing the failure is permanent and rigid.

To keep everything unconditional and elementary we work with the doubling orbit $\{2^nP\}$, a subset of the set of all multiples; refuting the conjecture there refutes it as usually stated.

\begin{definition}[Duplication]
For $N \in \Z$ and $x \in \Q$ set
\[
\mathrm{dbl}_x(N,x) = \frac{x^4 - 8Nx}{4(x^3+N)}, \qquad
\mathrm{dbl}_y(N,x,y) = \frac{3x^2}{2y}\bigl(x - \mathrm{dbl}_x(N,x)\bigr) - y .
\]
These are the tangent-line formulas for $E_N$: the tangent at $(x,y)$ has slope $\lambda = 3x^2/(2y)$ and $x(2P) = \lambda^2 - 2x$, which simplifies to the displayed quotient using $y^2 = x^3+N$. We write $2^nP$ for the $n$-fold iterate of the resulting map on affine points.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Good and bad primes]
A prime $\ell$ is \emph{bad} for $E_N$ if $\ell \mid 6N$ and \emph{good} otherwise. Since $\Delta = -2^4 3^3 N^2$, the bad primes are exactly the prime divisors of $\Delta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The conjecture, formalised]
Say $(N,P)$ satisfies \emph{only bad primes} if for all $n \ge 0$ and every prime $\ell$ dividing the denominator of the $x$-coordinate of $2^nP$ we have $\ell \mid 6N$.
\end{definition}

\section{The rigid shape of denominators}

For $q \in \Q$ write $q = \num(q)/\den(q)$ in lowest terms with $\den(q) \ge 1$.

\begin{theorem}[Square--cube rigidity]
Let $N \in \Z$ and let $(x,y) \in \Q^2$ satisfy $y^2 = x^3+N$. Then $\den(y)^2 = \den(x)^3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For rational $q$ and $k \ge 1$, $\den(q^k) = \den(q)^k$, since numerator and denominator are coprime and hence so are their $k$-th powers; also $\den(q+m) = \den(q)$ for $m \in \Z$. Applying $\den$ to $y^2 = x^3+N$ gives $\den(y)^2 = \den(x^3+N) = \den(x)^3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $(e^2,e^3)$ parametrisation]
There is a unique integer $e \ge 1$ with $\den(x) = e^2$ and $\den(y) = e^3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $\den(x)^3 = \den(y)^2$ we get $\den(x)^2 \mid \den(y)^2$, hence $\den(x) \mid \den(y)$; write $\den(y) = \den(x)e$. Substituting and cancelling the nonzero factor $\den(x)^2$ gives $\den(x) = e^2$, hence $\den(y) = e^3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Integral model]
With $a = \num(x)$, $b = \num(y)$ and $e$ as above, $b^2 = a^3 + Ne^6$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}
For a rational point $(x,y)$ on $E_N$ and a prime $\ell$: $\ell \mid \den(x) \iff \ell \mid \den(y)$. Moreover $\ell \mid \den(x)$ implies $\ell^2 \mid \den(x)$ and $\ell^3 \mid \den(y)$.
\end{proposition}

Every denominator of an $x$-coordinate on a Mordell curve is thus a perfect square, and each prime in it occurs to even order $\ge 2$: $3136 = 56^2$, $25 = 5^2$, $4\ell^2 = (2\ell)^2$.

\section{What a denominator prime means: the reduction dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Reduction dichotomy]
Let $(x,y)$ be a rational point on $E_N$ and $\ell$ a prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\ell \nmid \den(x)$ there exist $X,Y \in \F_\ell$ with
$Y^2 = X^3 + \bar N$, $X \cdot \overline{\den(x)} = \overline{\num(x)}$ and $Y \cdot \overline{\den(y)} = \overline{\num(y)}$: the point reduces to an affine point of the reduced cubic over $\F_\ell$.
\item If $\ell \mid \den(x)$ no $X \in \F_\ell$ satisfies $X \cdot \overline{\den(x)} = \overline{\num(x)}$: the point has no affine reduction and reduces to $O$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently $\ell \mid \den(x)$ if and only if $P$ lies in the kernel of reduction at $\ell$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $x = a/e^2$, $y = b/e^3$ with $b^2 = a^3 + Ne^6$. If $\ell \nmid e^2$ then $\bar e \neq 0$, and $X = \bar a \bar e^{-2}$, $Y = \bar b \bar e^{-3}$ satisfy the reduced equation after dividing the reduced integral model by $\bar e^6$. If $\ell \mid \den(x)$ then $\overline{\den(x)} = 0$, so $X \cdot \overline{\den(x)} = \overline{\num(x)}$ forces $\ell \mid \num(x)$, contradicting coprimality.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why the conjecture had to fail]
The criterion makes no reference to $\Delta$. A prime enters a denominator when the point becomes the identity modulo that prime. For $P$ of infinite order and $\ell$ good, $P$ reduces into the finite group $E_N(\F_\ell)$, so some multiple of $P$ lies in the kernel of reduction; infinitely many good primes therefore occur among denominators of multiples of $P$. The conjecture conflated primes where the \emph{curve} degenerates with primes where the \emph{point} degenerates.
\end{remark}

\section{A counterexample machine, and explicit counterexamples}

\begin{lemma}[Integral duplication]
If $(x,y) \in \Z^2$ satisfies $y^2 = x^3+N$, then $x(2P) = \dfrac{x^4-8Nx}{4y^2}$ as a quotient of integers, not necessarily in lowest terms.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Counterexample criterion]
Let $(x,y) \in \Z^2$ with $y^2 = x^3+N$ and $y \neq 0$, and let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \mid y$ and $\ell \nmid 6N$. Then $\ell \mid \den(x(2P))$. In particular $\ell$ is a prime of good reduction occurring in a denominator, so $(N,P)$ does not satisfy ``only bad primes''.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $x(2P) = A/B$ with $A = x^4-8Nx$, $B = 4y^2$; then $\ell \mid B$. If also $\ell \nmid A$, cross-multiplication $\num(q)B = A\den(q)$ for $q = A/B$ gives $\ell \mid \den(q)$. Now $\ell \nmid 6N$ gives $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \nmid 3$; from $\ell \mid y$ we get $\ell \mid y^2$, and $\ell \mid x$ would give $\ell \mid y^2 - x^3 = N$, so $\ell \nmid x$. Finally $A = x(x^3-8N) = x(y^2-9N)$; if $\ell \mid A$ then $\ell \mid x$ (excluded) or $\ell \mid y^2-9N$, whence $\ell \mid 9N$, again excluded.
\end{proof}

Counterexamples are therefore generic: one needs only an integral point whose $y$-coordinate has a prime factor other than $2$, $3$ and the prime factors of $N$.

\begin{theorem}[The counterexample $N=55$]
Let $N = 55 = 5\cdot 11$ and $P = (9,28) \in E_{55}(\Q)$, valid since $28^2 = 784 = 9^3+55$. Then
\[
x(2P) = \frac{9^4-8\cdot 55\cdot 9}{4(9^3+55)} = \frac{2601}{3136}, \qquad 3136 = 2^6\cdot 7^2 .
\]
The prime $7$ satisfies $7 \nmid 6\cdot 55 = 330$ and $7 \nmid \Delta = -432\cdot 55^2$, so a prime of good reduction divides a denominator and the conjecture is false. Moreover $5 \nmid 3136$ and $11 \nmid 3136$, so $\gcd(\den x(2P), N) = \gcd(3136,55) = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Permanence for $N=55$]
The equation $m^3+55 = 0$ has no integral solution, so $E_{55}$ has no rational point of order $2$ and the doubling orbit of $P = (9,28)$ never meets $O$. Consequently
\[
7 \mid \den\bigl(x(2^nP)\bigr) \ \text{ and } \ v_7\bigl(\den x(2^nP)\bigr) = 2 \qquad \text{for every } n \ge 1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A rational point with $y = 0$ has $\den(y) = 1$, hence $e = 1$ and $x \in \Z$ with $x^3+55 = 0$; bounding $m$ using the factorisations of $m^3+55$ near $m = -5$ and $m = -2$ confines $m$ to $-4 \le m \le -3$, where no solution exists. Persistence and rigidity then follow from the results of Section~6, starting from $v_7(3136) = 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A second counterexample]
Let $N = 33 = 3\cdot 11$ and $P = (-2,5)$, valid since $5^2 = (-2)^3+33$. Then $x(2P) = 136/25$, so $\den(x(2P)) = 25 = 5^2$ and the good prime $5$ (indeed $5 \nmid 198$) divides the denominator, while the factorisation $33 = 3\cdot 11$ is invisible.
\end{theorem}

\section{An infinite family of counterexamples}

\begin{theorem}[The family $N = \ell^2-1$]
Let $\ell \ge 5$ be prime and set $N = \ell^2-1$, $P = (1,\ell)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $P \in E_N(\Q)$, since $\ell^2 = 1^3 + (\ell^2-1)$;
\item $x(2P) = \dfrac{9-8\ell^2}{4\ell^2}$ in lowest terms, so $\den(x(2P)) = 4\ell^2$ exactly;
\item $\ell$ is a prime of good reduction: $\ell \nmid 6$ and $\ell \nmid \ell^2-1$;
\item hence $(N,P)$ fails ``only bad primes'';
\item no odd prime factor $r$ of $N = (\ell-1)(\ell+1)$ divides $\den(x(2P))$;
\item $\ell$ occurs with multiplicity exactly $2$: $\ell^2 \mid 4\ell^2$ but $\ell^3 \nmid 4\ell^2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(2) The duplication formula gives numerator $1-8(\ell^2-1) = 9-8\ell^2$ and denominator $4(1+\ell^2-1) = 4\ell^2$. A common prime factor $r$ divides $(9-8\ell^2)+2(4\ell^2) = 9$, so $r = 3$; but $3 \mid 4\ell^2$ forces $3 \mid \ell$, impossible for prime $\ell \ge 5$. (3) $\ell \mid 6$ is impossible, and $\ell \mid \ell^2-1$ would give $\ell \mid 1$. (5) An odd prime dividing $4\ell^2$ equals $\ell$, and $\ell \nmid N$. (6) is clear since $\ell \nmid 4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Infinitely many counterexamples]
The set $\{\,N \in \Z : \exists\, P \in E_N(\Q) \text{ with } (N,P) \text{ failing ``only bad primes''}\,\}$ is infinite.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\ell \mapsto \ell^2-1$ is injective on primes $\ell \ge 5$, there are infinitely many such primes, and each image carries a counterexample point.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$\ell = 7$: $N = 48$, $P = (1,7)$, $x(2P) = -383/196$ with $196 = 2^2\cdot 7^2$. $\ell = 11$: $N = 120$, $P = (1,11)$, $x(2P) = -959/484$ with $484 = 2^2\cdot 11^2$. The odd prime factors of $N$ are entirely absent in both cases.
\end{example}

\begin{corollary}[What the gcd returns]
For this family, every prime dividing $\gcd(\den x(2P), |N|)$ equals $2$: the gcd never exposes an odd factor of $N$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Exact $\ell$-adic dynamics along the doubling orbit}

\begin{theorem}[Persistence]
Let $(x,y)$ be a rational point on $E_N$ and $\ell$ a prime with $\ell \mid \den(x)$. Then $\ell \mid \den(x(2P))$; consequently a prime present at some stage of a doubling orbit (in which no point has $y = 0$) is present at every later stage.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $x = a/e^2$, $y = b/e^3$, $b^2 = a^3+Ne^6$, the duplication formula becomes the integral presentation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:star}
x(2P) = \frac{a^4-8Nae^6}{4b^2e^2}.
\end{equation}
Here $\ell \mid \den(x) = e^2$ gives $\ell \mid e$, so $\ell$ divides the denominator in \eqref{eq:star}; and $\ell$ dividing the numerator would force $\ell \mid a^4$, hence $\ell \mid a = \num(x)$, contradicting coprimality. Conceptually: the kernel of reduction is a subgroup, hence closed under doubling.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Valuation under duplication]
Let $(x,y)$ be a rational point on $E_N$ and $\ell$ a prime with $\ell \mid \den(x)$. Then
\[
v_\ell\bigl(\den x(2P)\bigr) = v_\ell(4) + v_\ell\bigl(\den x\bigr).
\]
Hence for odd $\ell$ the multiplicity is exactly preserved, while for $\ell = 2$ it grows by exactly two.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In \eqref{eq:star} the numerator is prime to $\ell$, so the valuation of the reduced denominator equals $v_\ell(4b^2e^2)$. Since $\ell \mid e \mid \den(y)$ we have $\ell \nmid b$, so this equals $v_\ell(4) + 2v_\ell(e) = v_\ell(4) + v_\ell(\den x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact entry of a good prime]
Let $(x,y)$ be a rational point on $E_N$ with $y \neq 0$, and let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \nmid 6N$ and $\ell \nmid \den(x)$. Then
\[
v_\ell\bigl(\den x(2P)\bigr) = 2\,v_\ell\bigl(\num y\bigr).
\]
In particular $\ell \mid \den(x(2P))$ if and only if $\ell \mid \num(y)$, and $\ell$ enters with the minimal multiplicity $2$ exactly when it divides $\num(y)$ exactly once.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use \eqref{eq:star} with $\ell \nmid e$. Writing $a^4-8Nae^6 = a(b^2-9Ne^6)$, the hypotheses $\ell \nmid 6N$, $\ell \nmid e$ exclude $\ell$ from both factors exactly as in the integral criterion. Hence the valuation of the reduced denominator is $v_\ell(4b^2e^2) = 2v_\ell(b)$.
\end{proof}

This is the exact form of the counterexample mechanism: the good primes that enter are dictated by the numerator of the $y$-coordinate, not by $N$. The integral criterion of Section~4 is the special case $e = 1$.

\begin{theorem}[Constancy along the orbit]
Suppose $x^3+N = 0$ has no integral solution, let $P$ be a rational point, let $\ell$ be an odd prime with $\ell \mid \den x(2^nP)$. Then $v_\ell(\den x(2^{n+m}P)) = v_\ell(\den x(2^nP))$ for all $m \ge 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness]
If $\ell$ is odd, $\ell \mid \den(x)$ and $v_\ell(\den x) = 2$---the minimum allowed by the $(e^2,e^3)$ shape---then $\ell^2 \mid \den x(2P)$ and $\ell^3 \nmid \den x(2P)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Formal group interpretation]
These statements are the elementary shadow of the theory of formal groups: the kernel of reduction at $\ell$ is filtered by subgroups on whose graded pieces multiplication by $2$ acts by the multiplier $2$, a unit exactly when $\ell$ is odd. Duplication is therefore an automorphism of the filtration for odd $\ell$ and deepens it by $v_\ell(4) = 2$ when $\ell = 2$.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}[The dynamics in action]
For $N = 55$, $P = (9,28)$ the denominators of $x(2^nP)$ are perfect squares $e_n^2$ with
\[
e_1^2 = 2^6\cdot 7^2, \quad e_2^2 = 2^8\cdot 7^2\cdot 827^2\cdot 1583^2, \quad
e_3^2 = 2^{10}\cdot 7^2\cdot 827^2\cdot 1583^2\cdot 125017^2\cdots
\]
Every prediction is visible: $v_2$ increases by exactly $2$ per step; the good prime $7$ is frozen at exponent $2$; new good primes enter with exponent $2$ and persist; and $5,11 \nmid e_n$ throughout.
\end{example}

\section{Cryptographic consequences: a denominator barrier}

The motivation for the conjecture was the map $P \mapsto g_n(N,P) := \gcd(\den x(2^nP), N)$, hoped to be a factor-revealing oracle.

\begin{proposition}
If no prime factor of $N$ divides $d$, then $\gcd(d,N) = 1$. In particular $g_1 = \gcd(3136,55) = 1$ for $N = 55$, $P = (9,28)$, and for the family $N = \ell^2-1$, $P = (1,\ell)$ every prime dividing $g_1$ equals $2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Denominator barrier]
A prime $r \mid N$ divides $\den(x(2^nP))$ if and only if $2^nP$ lies in the kernel of reduction at $r$. Since the primes dividing $N$ are primes of bad reduction, this is a condition on the reduction of the point in the singular fibre at $r$, not a consequence of any property of $N$ as a product. The denominator sequence is computed from $N$ as a single integer input and hence carries, by itself, no information distinguishing $N = pq$ from any other integer of the same size.
\end{theorem}

If $N$ is squarefree and $r \mid N$ is odd, then modulo $r$ the curve becomes $y^2 = x^3$, a cuspidal cubic whose smooth locus is $\Ga(\F_r) \cong (\F_r,+)$. For odd $r$ this group is uniquely $2$-divisible with no element of order $2$, so a doubling orbit inside it cannot reach the identity unless it starts there. The primes of $N$ are protected by the geometry of the bad fibre.

\paragraph{Experimental corroboration.} For every semiprime $N = pq < 200$ carrying a small integral point we computed the primes appearing in $\den(x(2^nP))$ for $n \le 3$, over $25$ curves.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Statistic & Result \\
\midrule
``Only bad primes'' holds & $0$ of $25$ ($0\%$) \\
Larger prime $q$ appears in a denominator & $0$ of $25$ ($0\%$) \\
Smaller prime $p$ appears in a denominator & $10$ of $25$ ($40\%$, almost always $p \in \{2,3\}$) \\
Every denominator is a perfect square & $25$ of $25$ ($100\%$, as required) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The intruder good primes are typically large: for $N = 55$ they are $7, 827, 1583, 125017, \dots$; for $N = 15$ they are $263, 1499, 151681, 6351673, \dots$ These are exactly the primes dividing the successive numerators $\num y(2^nP)$, as the entry theorem predicts.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Denominator orbit.} Given $N$, $P = (x_0,y_0)$ and $n$, apply $x \mapsto (x^4-8Nx)/(4(x^3+N))$ in exact rational arithmetic, recording $\den(x)$ at each step. Numerators and denominators roughly quadruple in bit-length each step (canonical heights multiply by $4$), so step $n$ costs $O(M(4^nH))$ bit operations. Denominators are always perfect squares $e_n^2$, halving the size of the integers to be factored.

\paragraph{Good-prime entry test.} Given an integral point $(x,y)$ on $E_N$, output any prime $\ell \mid y$ with $\ell \nmid 6N$. Each such $\ell$ is a certified good prime dividing $\den(x(2P))$; no doubling and no large-integer factorisation is required, turning counterexample search into a linear scan over small integral points.

\paragraph{Denominator gcd probe.} Compute $g_n = \gcd(\den x(2^nP), N)$ for $n = 1,\dots,n_{\max}$ and report nontrivial values. Its systematic failure is the content of the barrier.

\section{Discussion and open problems}

\subsection{What replaces the conjecture}

The correct statement is point-theoretic, not curve-theoretic. Denominators of $x$-coordinates on $E_N$ are perfect squares $e^2$; a prime divides $e$ exactly when the point lies in the kernel of reduction there; along a doubling orbit each odd prime's multiplicity is frozen from the moment of entry, the prime $2$ gains exactly two per step, and the primes entering at step $n+1$ are exactly the good primes dividing $\num y(2^nP)$, with multiplicity twice their multiplicity in that numerator. This describes the denominator sequence completely in terms of the numerator sequence, which satisfies an elliptic divisibility-type recursion.

\subsection{Open problems}

\begin{problem}[Squarefree entry]
Let $P$ be non-torsion and $y_n = y(2^nP)$. Is it true that every good prime entering at step $n+1$ (i.e.\ $\ell \mid \num y_n$, $\ell \nmid e_n$) satisfies $\ell^2 \nmid \num y_n$, for all but finitely many $\ell$ depending on $(N,P)$? Since the entry multiplicity is exactly $2v_\ell(\num y_n)$ and thereafter constant, the whole question of denominator shape reduces to squarefreeness of an explicit integer recursion---accessible to sieve methods and settled by $abc$-type input. Falsification test: for $N \le 200$, small integral points and $n \le 4$, factor $\num y_n$ and look for a repeated good prime.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Total invisibility of the factorisation]
Are there infinitely many semiprimes $N = pq$ carrying an integral point $P$ with $y \neq 0$ such that no prime factor of $N$ ever divides $\den x(2^nP)$ for any $n \ge 1$? Step $1$ is already a theorem for the family $N = \ell^2-1$; extending it to all steps requires only the additive-reduction analysis of the same explicit fraction $(a^4-8Nae^6)/(4b^2e^2)$ together with the cuspidal-fibre argument.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Density of intruder primes]
For fixed $(N,P)$, how many good primes $\ell \le X$ occur in some denominator $\den x(2^nP)$? Heuristically this is governed by the order of the reduced point in $E_N(\F_\ell)$ being a power of $2$, suggesting asymptotics analogous to Lang--Trotter statistics and to primitive-divisor counts for elliptic divisibility sequences.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Beyond doubling]
For a general multiple $mP$ with $m$ odd, the expected analogue is: an odd prime $\ell \neq m$ already present keeps its multiplicity, while $\ell = m$ gains $2v_\ell(m)$. Proving this elementarily requires an integral presentation of the general addition formula.
\end{problem}

\subsection{Conclusion}

The ``only bad primes'' conjecture for Mordell curves is false, for the most basic possible reason: the primes appearing in denominators measure the degeneration of the \emph{point}, whereas the discriminant measures the degeneration of the \emph{curve}. The refutation is witnessed by the single fraction $x(2P) = 2601/3136$ on $E_{55}$ at $P = (9,28)$, whose denominator $2^6\cdot 7^2$ contains a prime of good reduction and is coprime to $N = 55$; it extends to the infinite family $N = \ell^2-1$, $P = (1,\ell)$ with denominator exactly $4\ell^2$; and it is completed by an exact determination of the $\ell$-adic dynamics. What remains is not a factoring algorithm but a clean and predictive theory of Mordell denominators---and a precise reason why that theory carries no information about the factorisation of $N$.

